{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/planning/C45-draft.md docs/planning/verification-policy.md docs/planning/chunks/C45.md'","aggregated_output":"# C45 — One presentation, one identity (P10)\n\nOwner decisions settled on 2026-11-01 through the grill-me rounds. This brief\nsupersedes the 2026-10-31 draft. Required predecessor: P09 phase review passing\nat 9, after C44 and the L19-dependent C29–C32 reviews. C44 attempt-05 has now\npassed at 9 in every dimension and is merged. L19 passed; C29 attempt-07 was\naccepted at 8, its missing native coverage has been restored in the driver,\nand fresh native execution plus C29–C32/P09 reviews remain pending in Worf.\nThis approved planning brief is retained before the VM move; C45 implementation\nhas not started. Proposed decision id D061 must be checked for availability then.\n\n## Confirmed outcome\n\n- Display name: **Prisma AIRS Desktop**, with the version in About.\n- Release version: **1.0.0**, replacing the owner's pending 2.0.0 version bump.\n- Production identity and protected-data prefix: **io.cdot.airs-desktop**.\n- Fixture identity: **io.cdot.airs-desktop.fixture**; fixture bundle name\n  **Prisma AIRS Desktop Fixture**.\n- **Fresh setup.** No automatic migration or credential adoption from either the\n  alpha or the current v2 development installation. Their data and Keychain items\n  remain intact. There is no one-way v2-to-v1 migration in this chunk.\n- **P10** contains C45 and closes before C26's signed release. C45 follows D034:\n  accept at 8 with no blockers, record and remediate findings, then raise every\n  score to 9. P10 requires its own cumulative phase review at 9, with no required\n  fixes, blockers or evidence gaps and no owner acceptance of the phase.\n\nThe earlier decisions remain: remove the legacy presentation and Rust variant\nmachinery entirely; remove every alpha-import surface (D059, D060). The new\npresentation preserves the working behavior of active Settings, Sessions,\nDiagnostics and Academy helpers while replacing or moving their implementation.\n\n## Repository facts that correct the earlier draft\n\nA TypeScript import-graph walk from `apps/desktop/src/main.tsx` found 19 live\nmodules under `features/`. Eight files in the old deletion list are still live:\n\n- `academy/desktop-link.ts`, `academy/host-actions.ts`\n- `diagnostics/diagnostics-panel.tsx`\n- `sessions/sessions-model.ts`, `sessions/sessions-view.tsx`\n- `tenants/credential-entry.tsx`, `tenants/profile-import.tsx`,\n  `tenants/tenants-settings.tsx`\n\nSettings and Sessions are mounted through the current shell. Their behavior must\nbe ported before their old views are deleted. The profile-import surface is an\nintentional D060 removal; the surrounding tenant and credential behavior remains.\nThe Academy truth-graph test is not a runtime import and must retain its coverage\nunder a new path rather than being deleted merely because it is unreachable\nfrom the production entry point.\n\nThe installed alpha is `/Applications/Prisma AIRS Desktop.app`, identifier\n`io.cdot.prisma-airs-desktop`, version 0.11.11. Its Academy Keychain namespace is\n`io.cdot.prisma-airs-desktop.academy`. The old draft's unsuffixed identifier would\ncollide with it. The chosen `io.cdot.airs-desktop` namespace avoids that collision.\nThe display-name installation-path collision belongs to the later C26 install\nplan and its signing/release grilling session; building C45 does not install over\nthat app.\n\nResearch inventory: `evidence/C45/import-graph-2026-11-01.json`. Recompute the\nimport graph on the merged P09 tree before deleting files.\n\n## Implementation order\n\n1. Create an isolated C45 worktree from the completed P09 integration. Inventory\n   owner modifications and preserve them. Stage explicit paths only. Archive\n   named legacy edits that the owner explicitly retired before removing them;\n   preserve unrelated owner core, build, release and shared-helper work.\n2. Port the active Settings, Sessions and Diagnostics views to the one\n   presentation. Move the live Academy, administration, Runtime, tenant and\n   workbench helpers out of `features/`, preserving their compiled behavior.\n   Keep the Academy truth and hosted-authority guards under maintained test\n   paths. Delete dead views and superseded tests after their behavior is covered\n   by the one presentation. Remove the two untracked legacy twins only after\n   confirming that no maintained module imports them. The `features/` directory\n   ends empty and is removed.\n3. Remove `UiVariant`, per-variant catalogues/default navigation, the stored flag,\n   `SetUiVariant`, the snapshot field, generated types and variant-only fixtures\n   and tests. Remove the renderer's legacy-layout context once its consumers are\n   ported. There is one catalogue and one presentation.\n4. Remove the alpha reader and adapters, `V1Import*` intents/views, Settings'\n   tenant-profile file import and API Client profile adoption, their discovery\n   paths, and import-only fixtures/tests. Ordinary user-selected API collection\n   files keep their current format and editor behavior. This is not a file-format\n   rewrite.\n5. Apply the chosen production/fixture identities, version and namespaces across\n   Tauri/Rust/package metadata, native menus, About, storage, credential services,\n   fixture setup, drivers and current documentation. Production state uses\n   `prisma-airs-desktop/state/1` in the new application's data root. Start fresh\n   without reading or deleting alpha/v2 roots, credentials, sessions or approvals.\n   Update current live-tool naming without making live calls or silently changing\n   the owner's approved manifest. Historical verdicts, evidence and the frozen\n   prototype retain their provenance; checkout/remote paths remain stable.\n6. Register C45/P10 in the controller, checks, native inventory, review schema,\n   plan and status. Record the owner-approved sequencing: P10 follows P09 and\n   precedes the signed P08 release. If C26 still blocks controller transport,\n   retain the explicit fresh-exec fallback limitation rather than inventing\n   controller receipts or treating C26 as completed.\n\n## Gate and evidence\n\nDuring implementation: typecheck, affected Vitest suites and Rust tests. Resolve\ncontracts from the active checkout, including when dependencies are shared across\nworktrees. Preserve the C44 scope and commit-phase editor regressions.\n\nBefore the heavy gate, verify the production import graph has no `features/`\nconsumer, old variant/import contracts are gone, protected namespaces are distinct,\nand current names/version are consistent. Fixture tests seed old alpha/v2 canaries\nin synthetic roots and prove that fresh v1 setup neither imports nor purges them.\nThey never inspect real old credentials to prove this.\n\nAfter focused preflight and cross-consumer review settle, rebuild the renamed fixture and run ONE justified broad identity/isolation/cleanup baseline: the **full current cumulative set**, including restored C32 and new C45 assertions:\nlegacy C03, C04, C05, C06, C07, C10, C11, C24, C31 through the adapter and C35–C43,\nplus the whole-app C44 checks and new C45 identity/fresh-setup checks. Bind every\nrequired assertion to the exact executable. The assertion-level map remains the\nsource of truth: a replaced legacy aggregate such as C24-N06 is proved by the\ncurrent whole-app aggregate, not demanded from the obsolete legacy matrix.\nAny new retirement needs an owner decision; the accepted retirement list is not\nsilently expanded.\n\nParameterize reused drivers so C45 records/captures land under `evidence/C45/`.\nKeep P09/C44 evidence intact. Run the full source-bound check suite, freeze an\nattempt folder that has no verdict, build a self-excluding review packet, and\nobtain the fresh read-only Codex review. Record the same-model-family limitation\nand actual implementer/orchestrator. Verify hashes, run logs and native bindings\nagain after the verdict. Report every score immediately and retain every failure.\n\nRaise C45 to 9, integrate its tree while preserving owner work, execute checks affected by actual merge changes and obtain the P10 phase review at9. Under [the owner verification policy](verification-policy.md), unchanged qualified artifacts/helper closures retain their evidence; P10 consumes the same qualified candidate when valid. Remediation, a new review or a merge alone never repeats the full baseline or Rust suite. The signed C26 build then qualifies\nthat integrated tree under the owner's live/signing scope.\n\n## Outside this chunk\n\nC29's L19 designation and live row, P09's phase pass, C25 Academy live approvals,\nC26 signing/notarization/install decisions, and publishing. They retain their\nexisting owner gates. The installed alpha's app-name collision is resolved in\nthe C26 installation plan. No push without the owner's word.\n\n## Current activation and verification policy — 2026-11-02\n\nActual P09attempt02 passed9/9/9/9/9 and its audit; activation proof is retained in\nevidence/C45/activation-20261102-g11/p09-proof.json. Earlier pending-stage wording\nabove records the original approved brief. The owner's risk-based policy in\nverification-policy.md governs current C45/P10 scheduling: one broad baseline,\nthen causal/affected checks and strict explicit evidence reuse/carry. All required\ncoverage is accounted, original raw bindings stay immutable, and actual C45/P10\nindependent all9 reviews remain required. No release/live authority is added.\n# Risk-based verification strategy — owner direction, 2026-11-02\n\nStatus: future policy, including C45/P10. Record and implement it before the next phase's qualification. Do not interrupt, change or retroactively reinterpret the active P09 workflow, its sealed inputs, reviews or historical outcomes.\n\nThe owner wants materially faster iteration while preserving confidence in the backend/frontend migration. Full end-to-end qualification is reserved for changes that warrant it. The earlier 2026-11-01 exact-artifact verification-reuse decision remains in force.\n\n## Select verification from the change\n\nBefore expensive work, write a short verification plan: changed behavior and components, authority/data/platform boundaries touched, affected tests/native flows, retained evidence and its justification, uncertainty, and the reason for any full run. Keep this a small record using the existing runner, not a new orchestration framework.\n\n1. During implementation, run meaningful focused unit and production-interface integration regressions, relevant typechecks, and affected lint/format checks. Cover failure ordering, teardown and stale ownership at this layer first. Rust test-only or lint-only changes require their relevant targets, not an unrelated workspace or native suite.\n2. For changed user-facing or native behavior, exercise the affected native flows and boundaries. Include related consumers of shared changed components. Use a small startup/navigation/critical-action smoke set when integration or packaging makes it useful.\n3. Run the full end-to-end matrix when justified by broad architectural/runtime migration; wide native bridge, shared authority or lifecycle changes; application identity, protected storage or install changes; release-candidate qualification; or impact/evidence uncertainty that cannot be bounded confidently.\n4. A new commit, worktree, merge, review attempt, review score, timestamp or source fingerprint does not by itself justify full re-execution. Decide from actual behavior, dependencies, artifact and execution-condition changes.\n\nWhen a bug reveals a shared invariant, inspect all its relevant callers and add cross-consumer production-path regressions before sealing. For example, test native confirmation ownership at admission, queued presentation, shown presentation and retirement across applicable callers together. Catch the class of failure before another long UI run.\n\nValidate native assertion maps and their positive/negative prerequisites cheaply. Loading, errors or incomplete screens cannot prove that a successful detail surface lacks forbidden actions.\n\n## Preserve and reuse evidence honestly\n\n- For exact reuse, verify the actual executable and complete canonical app bundle, relevant per-driver helper/import closure, build flags, and execution conditions against the original passing evidence. Run newly affected checks only. A source-input hash change alone must not force native or Rust suite repetition.\n- When application behavior or relevant artifact bytes change, execute affected checks on the current candidate. Unaffected historical coverage may be carried with an explicit dependency/impact justification and original artifact binding; do not call it a current execution or assert byte equality that does not hold. Broaden testing when that justification is uncertain.\n- For an isolated native driver/helper change, run its coverage validators and affected driver consumers. Retain other valid completed drivers when their own helper closure, app artifact and relevant conditions remain valid.\n- Every required assertion needs accounted coverage. Distinguish executed now, reused with exact proof, carried unaffected with rationale, not applicable with reason, and explicit owner-accepted risk. An assertion cannot disappear through omission or a score change.\n- Preserve raw logs, failures, verdicts, source inventories, hashes and original run records unchanged. New proof references them; it never rewrites their provenance or turns an unperformed check into a pass.\n- Missing or inconsistent proof requires the affected verification. New requirement retirements and accepted risks retain their existing owner-decision rules.\n\n## Coalesce expensive work\n\nFinish focused checks, source/interface review and cross-consumer risk inspection before freezing a candidate. Resolve known lint, typing, contract and fixture/assertion problems before starting the long matrix.\n\nQualify one stable candidate at the justified integration boundary. Let chunk and phase reviews consume that same qualified artifact and evidence when validity is preserved. A formal review still examines the actual current source and evidence and reports its real scores; it does not automatically schedule another full test run.\n\nFor review fixes, execute the causal regression and affected checks, then update the impact/evidence record. Remediation or merge triggers another full run only when its actual risk warrants one. Do not reopen unaffected chunks or repeat unrelated checks merely to refresh source provenance.\n\nKeep long native jobs durable and independent of model service lifetime, with one UI writer, ownership/source guards, per-step immutable results and a resumable queue. Recover from infrastructure failure at the first unfinished valid step. Do not restart completed valid steps.\n\n## Apply concretely to C45/P10\n\nC45's new application/Keychain/data identities, fresh-setup isolation and broad presentation cleanup warrant one complete current cumulative native baseline, including new identity/isolation assertions, after implementation and focused preflight settle. Preserve all P09 evidence.\n\nAfter that baseline, use affected checks for C45 fixes. P10 may consume the same qualified candidate rather than automatically repeating the full C44/C45 matrix and Rust suite. Integration gets checks for actual merge changes; an unchanged qualified artifact and helper closure reuse the existing proof.\n\nAfter P09 has passed, copy this policy into the isolated C45 worktree at docs/planning/verification-policy.md. Update the C45 brief's blanket rerun wording, repository guidance, test catalogue, review protocol and new P10 provenance/gates to implement this owner direction explicitly. Keep historical P09 verifiers and results unchanged. Keep implementation small: a change-impact/verification-plan record and strict validation of selected checks and evidence reuse.\n\nRetain existing chunk/phase independent-review and release requirements, including separately recorded owner exceptions. This strategy changes verification scope and scheduling; it does not fabricate scores, bypass assertion coverage, grant live access, or authorize signing/publication.\n# C45 — One presentation, one identity (P10)\n\nImplement the approved C45-draft.md under D060/D061 after actualP09all9 activation. Preserve active Settings/Sessions/Diagnostics/Academy helpers, shared file-picker types, type-onlyCasePrefill and C44 editor scope/commit-phase tests. Remove legacy views, variant machinery, alpha/tenant-profile/API-profile-adoption readers/contracts. Keep ordinary collection file formats.\n\nUse Prisma AIRS Desktop1.0.0/io.cdot.airs-desktop, distinct fixtureio.cdot.airs-desktop.fixture/Prisma AIRS Desktop Fixture, stateprisma-airs-desktop/state/1 and separate protected services. Fresh setup never reads/adopts/purges prior alpha/v2 stores/credentials; tests use controlled canaries only.\n\nFollow verification-policy.md/D062: focused regressions/typechecks/lint and cross-consumer preflight, then ONE justified broad current mapped20+C45identity/isolation baseline. Followups/merge/P10 use affected verification and strictly accounted evidence reuse/carry. Actual independent chunk >=8 without blockers may be acceptedD034 with findings preserved; raise toactualall9 before actualP10phaseall9. No phase owner acceptance, live calls, publication, signing or installation. C26/P08 remains open with explicit manual fresh-read-only review fallback.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
